window.NET_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is len(\"naïve\".encode(\"utf-8\")) ?","o":["5","6","7","10"],"a":1,"w":"Five code points, but ï (U+00EF) is outside ASCII, so UTF-8 encodes it in 2 bytes: 4 × 1 + 2 = 6."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Bytes 2–3 of a TCP header (the destination port) are 01 bb . Which port is it?","o":["187","47873","80","443"],"a":3,"w":"Header fields are big-endian: 0x01BB = 1 × 256 + 187 = 443. Reading them little-endian would give 0xBB01 = 47873, the classic forgotten- ntohs bug."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A service displays a customer's name as \"JosÃ©\". What most likely happened?","o":["UTF-8 bytes were decoded with a single-byte encoding such as Latin-1","The network corrupted the packet","The bytes were sent little-endian","The name was truncated by Content-Length"],"a":0,"w":"é is c3 a9 in UTF-8. Latin-1 maps c3 to \"Ã\" and a9 to \"©\". Single-byte decoders never fail, so the bug shows up as garbage text instead of an error."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Ignoring protocol overhead, what is the best-case transfer rate of a 100 Mbit/s link?","o":["100 MB/s","800 MB/s","12.5 MB/s","1.25 MB/s"],"a":2,"w":"Rates are quoted in bits. 100 Mbit/s ÷ 8 bits per byte = 12.5 MB/s, before headers, retransmissions and the rest of the stack take their share."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Bytes, Encoding & Byte Order","f":"p1-bytes-and-encoding.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does struct.pack(\"!I\", 0xC0A8010A) return?","o":["b'\\x0a\\x01\\xa8\\xc0'","b'\\xc0\\xa8\\x01\\x0a'","The string \"192.168.1.10\"","It depends on the CPU"],"a":1,"w":"! means network order, big-endian, on every CPU: most significant byte first. Those four bytes are how the IPv4 address 192.168.1.10 appears in a packet header ( P2 )."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What 32-bit integer is the address 0.0.1.0 ?","o":["1","16","256","65,536"],"a":2,"w":"The third octet is worth 2⁸ = 256 per unit, so 0.0.1.0 = 1 × 256 = 256. (0.1.0.0 would be 65,536.)"},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the subnet mask for a /20 prefix?","o":["255.255.240.0","255.255.255.240","255.255.20.0","255.240.0.0"],"a":0,"w":"20 ones: 8 + 8 fill the first two octets with 255, and the remaining 4 ones make the third octet 11110000 = 240."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which network does 172.20.47.200/20 belong to?","o":["172.20.47.0","172.20.32.0","172.20.0.0","172.20.40.0"],"a":1,"w":"The mask splits the third octet: 47 AND 240 = 00101111 AND 11110000 = 00100000 = 32. Equivalently, blocks of 16, and 47 is in the block starting at 32."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which of these addresses is not in a private (RFC 1918) range?","o":["10.255.0.1","172.31.200.7","192.168.100.100","172.32.0.1"],"a":3,"w":"172.16.0.0/12 runs from 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255. 172.32.0.1 is one past the end, so it's a public address."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"IP Addresses & Binary Arithmetic","f":"p2-ip-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Host 10.0.1.20/24 sends a packet to 10.0.2.9. What does it do?","o":["ARPs for 10.0.2.9 and sends directly","Broadcasts the packet on the LAN","Sends it to its default gateway, because 10.0.2.9 is on another network","Drops it: 10.0.2.9 is unreachable"],"a":2,"w":"10.0.2.9 AND 255.255.255.0 = 10.0.2.0, which differs from the host's own network 10.0.1.0, so the packet goes to the gateway (whose MAC the host finds with ARP)."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which values identify one TCP connection?","o":["The server's IP address and port","Protocol, source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port","The client's MAC address and the server's port","The process ID of the server"],"a":1,"w":"The 5-tuple. Change any one value and it's a different connection, which is how many clients share one server port."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You open two browser tabs to the same HTTPS site. How does the server tell the two connections apart?","o":["It gives each tab a different server port","By a cookie in the first packet","The laptop's OS picked a different source port for each, so the 5-tuples differ","It can't; both tabs share one connection"],"a":2,"w":"Everything else is identical, so the source port is what makes each 5-tuple unique. (Browsers may also reuse one connection for both tabs, but if they open two, this is how they're told apart.)"},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What happens when a client sends a TCP SYN to a port where nothing is listening?","o":["The host replies with a reset (RST) and the client reports \"connection refused\"","The packet waits until a program listens","The next free port is used instead","The client always times out"],"a":0,"w":"A closed port is answered immediately with RST. A timeout usually means a firewall dropped the SYN silently, a useful difference when debugging ( P4 )."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With Linux's default ephemeral range, roughly how many simultaneous connections can one client IP open to 10.0.0.5:443 ?","o":["1,024","65,535","Unlimited","About 28,000"],"a":3,"w":"Only the source port can vary, and the default range 32768–60999 has 28,232 ports. To go further, add client IPs, server addresses or ports, or widen the range."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Clients, Servers & Ports","f":"p3-clients-servers-ports.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In ss -tan on a server, a line reads LISTEN 0 511 0.0.0.0:443 0.0.0.0:* . What is it?","o":["A client connected from 0.0.0.0","A listening socket accepting connections on port 443 on every interface","A connection waiting to close","A UDP socket"],"a":1,"w":"0.0.0.0 as the local address means \"all interfaces\", and * as the peer means there is no peer: it's a listener. 511 is the size of its queue of completed connections waiting for accept() ."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"curl prints Failed to connect to api.example.com port 443: Connection refused . What do you know?","o":["DNS failed","The host is reachable, but nothing is listening on port 443 there","A firewall silently dropped the packets","The certificate is invalid"],"a":1,"w":"\"Refused\" means a reset came back, so the packets reached a host that has no listening socket on that port. A silent drop would produce a timeout instead."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"ping to a host works, but curl to port 443 hangs and then reports a timeout. What's the most likely cause?","o":["The web server isn't running","DNS returned the wrong address","A firewall or security group drops TCP 443 while allowing ICMP","The TLS certificate expired"],"a":2,"w":"The host is reachable (ping), and nothing answers the SYN at all, not even a reset. Something between is dropping packets to that port."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"dig shows status: NXDOMAIN . What does it mean?","o":["The name does not exist","The DNS server is down","The name exists but has no A record","The answer was too large for UDP"],"a":0,"w":"NXDOMAIN is an authoritative \"no such name\". A name that exists without that record type gives NOERROR with an empty answer, and a resolver that can't get any answer returns SERVFAIL."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In curl -v output, what do lines starting with > show?","o":["Response headers","TLS handshake messages","Errors","The request line and headers curl sent"],"a":3,"w":"> is outgoing (the request), < is incoming (the response), and * is curl's own commentary on DNS, TCP and TLS."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"ping, curl, dig & Friends","f":"p4-network-cli-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A reply shows ttl=54 from a Linux server. Roughly how many routers did it cross?","o":["54","About 10","About 200","It's the round-trip time in ms"],"a":1,"w":"TTL is a hop counter, not a time. Linux starts at 64 and each router subtracts 1, so 64 − 54 = 10 hops (Windows starts at 128, many routers at 255)."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does srv.accept() return?","o":["The first bytes the client sent","True once a client is connected","A new socket for that one connection, and the client's (IP, port)","The listening socket, now connected"],"a":2,"w":"The listening socket keeps listening. Each accepted connection gets its own socket, which is the one you recv and send on."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A blocking TCP socket's recv(4096) returns b\"\" . What happened?","o":["The peer closed its side of the connection","No data has arrived yet; call again","The timeout expired","The buffer was too small"],"a":0,"w":"A blocking recv waits for data, so an empty result can only mean end of stream: the other side sent FIN. A timeout raises TimeoutError instead."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why use sendall(data) instead of send(data) ?","o":["sendall is faster","send doesn't work on TCP","sendall waits for the peer to acknowledge the data","send may queue only part of the data and return how many bytes it took; sendall loops until all of it is queued"],"a":3,"w":"When the kernel send buffer is nearly full, send takes what fits. Neither call waits for the peer's acknowledgement; both return once the bytes are in the local kernel's buffer."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A UDP client calls sendto(b\"ping\", (\"10.0.0.5\", 9001)) but nothing listens on that port. What does the client see?","o":["ConnectionRefusedError from sendto","sendto succeeds; the following recvfrom times out (if a timeout is set) or blocks forever","An empty datagram back","sendto blocks until a listener appears"],"a":1,"w":"UDP has no handshake, so sending can't discover that nobody is there. That's why UDP clients always need a timeout, and usually retries."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sockets in Python","f":"p5-python-sockets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Your server restarts and fails with OSError: [Errno 98] Address already in use , though the old process has exited. What helps?","o":["Using UDP instead","Calling listen() before bind()","Setting SO_REUSEADDR on the listening socket before bind()","A bigger listen backlog"],"a":2,"w":"Connections from the previous run are still in TIME_WAIT on that port. SO_REUSEADDR lets a new listening socket bind anyway; it doesn't let two live servers share the port (that's SO_REUSEPORT )."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You capture on a busy server and need the file to stay small. Which kind of filter achieves that?","o":["A capture (BPF) filter given to tcpdump","A Wireshark display filter","Either; they're the same thing","Neither; use -nn"],"a":0,"w":"Only a capture filter stops packets being recorded. A display filter hides packets in a file that already contains them."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which display filter shows only new connection attempts (the first packet of each handshake)?","o":["tcp.flags.syn == 1","tcp port 80","tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0","tcp.flags.ack == 0"],"a":2,"w":"The SYN-ACK also has SYN set, so syn == 1 alone shows both halves. Requiring ACK clear leaves only the client's opening SYN. tcp port 80 is BPF syntax."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In tcpdump output, what does Flags [P.] mean?","o":["A packet that was dropped","PSH and ACK: a segment carrying data, which also acknowledges","A ping","The last packet of the connection"],"a":1,"w":"P is PSH (deliver this data to the application now) and . is ACK. Most data segments look like this."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why add -nn to tcpdump?","o":["It captures full packets instead of headers","It enables IPv6","It prints packets in hex","It stops tcpdump resolving addresses to hostnames and ports to service names, so you see the real numbers and it doesn't make DNS lookups"],"a":3,"w":"Name resolution slows output, generates its own DNS traffic into your capture, and replaces 443 with \"https\". -X prints hex; -s 0 (the modern default) captures full packets."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Reading Packets with Wireshark","f":"p6-wireshark-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does Follow → TCP Stream show?","o":["The application bytes of one connection, reassembled in order, both directions","Every packet in the capture, in order","The route the packets took","Only the TCP headers"],"a":0,"w":"Wireshark puts the payloads of one 5-tuple's segments back in sequence order and shows client and server bytes in two colours. It also sets the display filter to tcp.stream eq N ."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In what order do headers appear in an HTTP request frame, from the first byte on the wire?","o":["HTTP, TCP, IP, Ethernet","Ethernet, IP, TCP, then the HTTP data","IP, Ethernet, TCP, HTTP","TCP, IP, Ethernet, HTTP"],"a":1,"w":"The outermost wrapper is added last by the sender and comes first on the wire: the link header, then IP, then TCP, then the application bytes."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A packet crosses an ordinary router (no NAT). What changes?","o":["Nothing at all","The TCP ports","The source and destination IP addresses","The Ethernet header is replaced, and the IP TTL is decremented (and the IPv4 checksum recomputed)"],"a":3,"w":"MAC addresses are per hop, so the frame is rebuilt. IP addresses and the transport header pass through unchanged, except for TTL and checksum."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With a 1500-byte MTU, IPv4 and a TCP header without options, what's the maximum TCP payload per segment?","o":["1500 bytes","1480 bytes","1460 bytes","1518 bytes"],"a":2,"w":"MSS = MTU − IP header (20) − TCP header (20) = 1460. With Linux's default 12-byte timestamp option it's 1448."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does a receiving host know the payload of an IP packet is TCP and not UDP?","o":["The IP header's Protocol field: 6 for TCP, 17 for UDP","It inspects the payload for a TCP header","From the destination port","From the Ethernet header's EtherType"],"a":0,"w":"Every header names its payload's type for the layer above. EtherType says \"IPv4\", IP's Protocol says \"TCP\", and the port says which socket."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Layering & Encapsulation","f":"p7-layering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After a VPN goes live, SSH and small requests work but large downloads hang. What's the most likely cause?","o":["The VPN encrypts large packets more slowly","A path MTU black hole: oversized packets are dropped and the ICMP \"fragmentation needed\" replies are blocked","TCP can't run inside a tunnel","DNS is failing for large responses"],"a":1,"w":"The tunnel lowers the MTU. Full-size packets with \"don't fragment\" set are dropped, and if ICMP is filtered the sender never learns to send smaller ones. Small packets fit, so interactive traffic still works."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Latency, Bandwidth & Throughput","f":"p8-latency-bandwidth.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What's the minimum one-way propagation delay over 2,000 km of fibre?","o":["0.1 ms","2 ms","10 ms","20 ms"],"a":2,"w":"About 5 µs per km (200,000 km/s): 2,000 × 5 µs = 10 ms one way, 20 ms round trip, before any queuing or route detours."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Latency, Bandwidth & Throughput","f":"p8-latency-bandwidth.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How long does it take to transmit one 1,500-byte packet onto a 100 Mbit/s link?","o":["120 µs","15 µs","1.5 ms","12 ms"],"a":0,"w":"1,500 × 8 = 12,000 bits ÷ 100,000,000 bits/s = 0.00012 s = 120 µs."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Latency, Bandwidth & Throughput","f":"p8-latency-bandwidth.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A connection's window is fixed at 64 KB and the RTT is 100 ms. What's the maximum throughput, whatever the link speed?","o":["64 KB/s","640 Mbit/s","Unlimited on a 10 Gbit/s link","About 5.2 Mbit/s"],"a":3,"w":"Throughput ≤ window ÷ RTT = 65,536 × 8 bits ÷ 0.1 s ≈ 5.2 Mbit/s. The link could be a thousand times faster and it wouldn't matter."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Latency, Bandwidth & Throughput","f":"p8-latency-bandwidth.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A page makes 30 small sequential requests to a server 100 ms away over a 50 Mbit/s connection. Which change helps most?","o":["Upgrading to 500 Mbit/s","Cutting the RTT, or the number of round trips (closer server, connection reuse, fewer sequential requests)","Compressing the responses","A faster server CPU"],"a":1,"w":"30 round trips × 100 ms = 3 s of waiting; transmitting a few KB each takes microseconds. Only fewer or shorter round trips move the total."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Latency, Bandwidth & Throughput","f":"p8-latency-bandwidth.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the bandwidth-delay product of a 1 Gbit/s path with a 40 ms RTT?","o":["40 Mbit","25 KB","5 MB","1 GB"],"a":2,"w":"10⁹ bits/s × 0.04 s = 4 × 10⁷ bits = 5 × 10⁶ bytes. TCP needs about 5 MB in flight to keep this path full."},{"n":1,"ti":"OSI vs TCP/IP","f":"01-osi-vs-tcp-ip.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"At which OSI layer does a router make its forwarding decision?","o":["Layer 1","Layer 2","Layer 3","Layer 4"],"a":2,"w":"Routers forward on the destination IP address, the network layer. They rebuild the L2 frame at each hop but never need to read L4."},{"n":1,"ti":"OSI vs TCP/IP","f":"01-osi-vs-tcp-ip.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which OSI layers does the TCP/IP model's application layer cover?","o":["5, 6 and 7","Only 7","4 to 7","6 and 7"],"a":0,"w":"Session, presentation and application are folded together, because in TCP/IP those jobs are done by applications and libraries rather than separate protocols."},{"n":1,"ti":"OSI vs TCP/IP","f":"01-osi-vs-tcp-ip.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What can an L7 load balancer do that an L4 load balancer cannot?","o":["Balance TCP connections across backends","Forward UDP traffic","Health-check backends","Route each HTTP request by its path or Host header"],"a":3,"w":"Only a balancer that terminates the connection and parses HTTP can see /api/orders or the Host header. An L4 balancer sees addresses and ports only."},{"n":1,"ti":"OSI vs TCP/IP","f":"01-osi-vs-tcp-ip.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A switch forwards a frame based on which field?","o":["The destination IP address","The destination MAC address","The destination port","The URL"],"a":1,"w":"A (layer 2) switch looks up the destination MAC in its MAC table to choose the output port ( Chapter 2 )."},{"n":1,"ti":"OSI vs TCP/IP","f":"01-osi-vs-tcp-ip.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why did the OSI model survive when the OSI protocols didn't?","o":["The internet still runs OSI's session layer","TCP/IP was replaced by OSI in the 1990s","Its numbered, finer-grained layers became the shared vocabulary for talking about networks","Routers implement all seven layers"],"a":2,"w":"TCP/IP won in deployment, but \"layer 2\", \"L4\" and \"L7\" are OSI numbers, used daily to describe devices, problems and attacks."},{"n":2,"ti":"Ethernet, MAC Addresses & Switches","f":"02-ethernet-switches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A switch receives a frame whose destination MAC isn't in its table. What does it do?","o":["Drops it","Floods it out of every port except the one it arrived on","Sends an ARP request","Returns it to the sender"],"a":1,"w":"Unknown unicast is flooded. The destination's reply then teaches the switch which port it's on."},{"n":2,"ti":"Ethernet, MAC Addresses & Switches","f":"02-ethernet-switches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which field of a frame does a switch learn from?","o":["The destination MAC","The EtherType","The source IP address","The source MAC"],"a":3,"w":"The source MAC tells the switch \"this host is reachable through the port this frame came in on\"."},{"n":2,"ti":"Ethernet, MAC Addresses & Switches","f":"02-ethernet-switches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which device ends a broadcast domain?","o":["A router","A switch","A hub","A network card"],"a":0,"w":"Routers don't forward L2 broadcasts. Switches and hubs pass them to every port, so everything behind them shares one broadcast domain (per VLAN)."},{"n":2,"ti":"Ethernet, MAC Addresses & Switches","f":"02-ethernet-switches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Two switches are connected by two cables and neither runs spanning tree. What happens when a host sends one ARP broadcast?","o":["The duplicate copy is dropped when its TTL reaches zero","Nothing unusual: switches detect duplicates","A broadcast storm: the frame circulates and multiplies forever, saturating the links","The second cable is ignored automatically"],"a":2,"w":"Ethernet frames have no TTL, so loops never drain. STP blocks redundant links to keep the topology a tree."},{"n":2,"ti":"Ethernet, MAC Addresses & Switches","f":"02-ethernet-switches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does the MAC address 02:42:ac:11:00:02 tell you?","o":["It's a broadcast address","It's locally administered (set by software), as Docker containers' addresses are","It belongs to a Cisco device","It's a multicast group"],"a":1,"w":"First byte 0x02: the multicast bit (0x01) is clear and the locally administered bit (0x02) is set. Docker assigns 02:42: followed by the container's IP."},{"n":3,"ti":"ARP: From IP to MAC","f":"03-arp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A laptop at 10.0.1.20/24 with gateway 10.0.1.1 sends a packet to 8.8.8.8. Its ARP cache is empty. What does it ARP for?","o":["8.8.8.8","10.0.1.1, the gateway","Nothing: ARP isn't used for internet traffic","Its DNS server"],"a":1,"w":"8.8.8.8 is off-subnet, so the frame goes to the gateway, and the laptop needs the gateway's MAC. The IP header still says 8.8.8.8."},{"n":3,"ti":"ARP: From IP to MAC","f":"03-arp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How are an ARP request and its reply addressed?","o":["Request broadcast to ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, reply unicast to the requester","Both broadcast","Both unicast","Request unicast to the router, reply broadcast"],"a":0,"w":"The requester doesn't know the target's MAC, so it must broadcast. The target knows the requester's MAC from the request, so it replies directly."},{"n":3,"ti":"ARP: From IP to MAC","f":"03-arp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is a gratuitous ARP typically used for?","o":["Resolving DNS names","Encrypting ARP traffic","Finding the default gateway","Announcing that an IP now lives at a new MAC, e.g. after a floating-IP failover"],"a":3,"w":"An unsolicited announcement updates every neighbour's cache (and switch's MAC table) at once, so traffic follows the IP to its new owner."},{"n":3,"ti":"ARP: From IP to MAC","f":"03-arp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You ping 192.168.1.77 on your own /24 and get \"From 192.168.1.23 icmp_seq=1 Destination Host Unreachable\", where .23 is you. What happened?","o":["A router on the internet rejected the packet","The remote host refused ICMP","Your ARP requests for .77 got no reply, so the packet was never sent","DNS failed"],"a":2,"w":"The error comes from your own address: your kernel gave up after unanswered ARP requests (the entry is FAILED in ip neigh )."},{"n":3,"ti":"ARP: From IP to MAC","f":"03-arp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does ARP spoofing let an attacker on the same LAN do?","o":["Decrypt TLS traffic directly","Redirect a victim's traffic through the attacker's machine by poisoning the victim's ARP cache","Change the victim's IP address","Take over the switch"],"a":1,"w":"Forged replies map the gateway's IP to the attacker's MAC. The attacker sees everything, but TLS traffic stays encrypted and can't be impersonated without a valid certificate."},{"n":4,"ti":"IP Addressing & Subnetting","f":"04-ip-subnetting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How many usable host addresses does a /26 have on an ordinary LAN?","o":["64","26","62","59"],"a":2,"w":"2 6 = 64 addresses, minus the network and broadcast addresses = 62. (On AWS it would be 59.)"},{"n":4,"ti":"IP Addressing & Subnetting","f":"04-ip-subnetting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the broadcast address of 192.168.10.64/27 ?","o":["192.168.10.255","192.168.10.95","192.168.10.127","192.168.10.91"],"a":1,"w":"A /27 is 32 addresses: .64 to .95. The broadcast is the last one, with every host bit set."},{"n":4,"ti":"IP Addressing & Subnetting","f":"04-ip-subnetting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What's the longest prefix (smallest subnet) that can hold 500 hosts?","o":["/24","/22","/25","/23"],"a":3,"w":"500 + 2 = 502 addresses needed. A /24 has 256, a /23 has 512, which is the smallest power of two that fits."},{"n":4,"ti":"IP Addressing & Subnetting","f":"04-ip-subnetting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which single route summarises 10.1.0.0/24, 10.1.1.0/24, 10.1.2.0/24 and 10.1.3.0/24 exactly?","o":["10.1.0.0/22","10.1.0.0/23","10.1.0.0/16","10.1.3.0/22"],"a":0,"w":"Four contiguous /24s starting at a multiple of 4 share their first 22 bits. /23 covers only two of them; /16 covers far more."},{"n":4,"ti":"IP Addressing & Subnetting","f":"04-ip-subnetting.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How many usable addresses does a /28 subnet give you in an AWS VPC?","o":["16","14","11","8"],"a":2,"w":"AWS reserves the first four addresses (network, VPC router, DNS, future use) and the last one: 16 − 5 = 11."},{"n":5,"ti":"Routing Tables & Forwarding","f":"05-routing-tables.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A router has routes 10.0.0.0/8 via A, 10.1.0.0/16 via B and 10.1.2.0/24 via C. Where does it send a packet for 10.1.2.3?","o":["A, the first route listed","B","C, the longest matching prefix","All three, for redundancy"],"a":2,"w":"All three match, and /24 is the longest. List order is irrelevant."},{"n":5,"ti":"Routing Tables & Forwarding","f":"05-routing-tables.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"With the same table, where does a packet for 10.9.9.9 go?","o":["A","B","C","It's dropped"],"a":0,"w":"Only 10.0.0.0/8 matches. Without that route and without a default route, it would be dropped with ICMP \"network unreachable\"."},{"n":5,"ti":"Routing Tables & Forwarding","f":"05-routing-tables.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the default route?","o":["The route with the lowest metric","The first route a router learned","A route to the local network","0.0.0.0/0: it matches every address, so it's used only when no more specific route exists"],"a":3,"w":"Prefix length 0 means no bits have to match. It's the least specific possible route."},{"n":5,"ti":"Routing Tables & Forwarding","f":"05-routing-tables.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A packet is caught in a routing loop between two routers. What eventually happens to it?","o":["It loops forever","Its TTL reaches 0, a router drops it and sends ICMP \"time exceeded\" to the source","The routers detect the loop and reroute it","It's delivered late"],"a":1,"w":"TTL exists for exactly this. Each hop subtracts 1; at 0 the packet is discarded. Unlike Ethernet frames, IP packets can't loop forever."},{"n":5,"ti":"Routing Tables & Forwarding","f":"05-routing-tables.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A client's SYN reaches the server, but the client's connection attempt times out. The server's routing table has no route back to the client's network. Why?","o":["The server rejects the SYN","The SYN was lost on the way","The server's SYN-ACK can't be routed back, so the handshake never completes","TTL expired on the SYN"],"a":2,"w":"The reply is a new packet with its own routing decision. With no matching route (and no default), the server drops it: the classic one-way routing failure."},{"n":6,"ti":"ICMP, ping & traceroute","f":"06-icmp-traceroute.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How does traceroute learn the address of the 4th router on a path?","o":["It sends a probe with TTL 4; the 4th router decrements it to 0 and replies with ICMP time exceeded from its own address","It asks the destination for its route","Each router appends its address to the probe","It reads the router's routing table"],"a":0,"w":"The router that expires the TTL reveals itself as the source of the time-exceeded message."},{"n":6,"ti":"ICMP, ping & traceroute","f":"06-icmp-traceroute.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"mtr shows 40% loss at hop 6, and 0% at hops 7 to 10 including the destination. What's going on?","o":["Hop 6 drops 40% of all traffic","The destination is overloaded","Hop 6 rate-limits the ICMP replies it generates; traffic through it is fine","The path changed during the test"],"a":2,"w":"If hop 6 dropped forwarded traffic, every later hop would show at least that loss. Loss that doesn't persist is about replies to probes aimed at that router."},{"n":6,"ti":"ICMP, ping & traceroute","f":"06-icmp-traceroute.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which ICMP message does path MTU discovery depend on?","o":["Echo reply","Time exceeded","Port unreachable","Destination unreachable, fragmentation needed (type 3, code 4)"],"a":3,"w":"A router that can't forward a DF-flagged packet sends type 3 code 4 with the next link's MTU. Block it and large transfers stall."},{"n":6,"ti":"ICMP, ping & traceroute","f":"06-icmp-traceroute.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A Linux traceroute ends in stars, but traceroute -T -p 443 to the same host completes. Why?","o":["TCP probes have a longer TTL","A firewall in front of the host drops UDP to high ports but allows TCP 443","The host is down for UDP","TCP traceroute uses a different route"],"a":1,"w":"Default UDP probes target ports 33434 and up. Firewalls usually allow only the service port, so a TCP SYN to 443 gets through and earns a SYN-ACK."},{"n":6,"ti":"ICMP, ping & traceroute","f":"06-icmp-traceroute.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How does UDP traceroute know it has reached the destination?","o":["The destination replies with ICMP port unreachable, since nothing listens on the probe's port","The destination sends a time-exceeded message","The TTL reaches 30","It receives a UDP reply"],"a":0,"w":"Probes go to unlikely high ports on purpose, so the destination's kernel answers with type 3 code 3 instead of time exceeded."},{"n":7,"ti":"UDP","f":"07-udp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How big is the UDP header?","o":["4 bytes","8 bytes","20 bytes","It varies with options"],"a":1,"w":"Source port, destination port, length and checksum, 16 bits each. UDP has no options."},{"n":7,"ti":"UDP","f":"07-udp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A sender calls sendto() three times with 100-byte messages. What can the receiver's recvfrom() calls return?","o":["One 300-byte result","Any split of the 300 bytes","Always exactly three 100-byte messages, in order","Up to three whole 100-byte messages, possibly fewer and possibly out of order"],"a":3,"w":"Boundaries are preserved, so each recvfrom returns one whole datagram, but any of them can be lost or reordered."},{"n":7,"ti":"UDP","f":"07-udp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why do voice and video calls use UDP rather than TCP?","o":["Late audio is useless, and TCP's retransmissions and in-order delivery would delay every frame behind a lost one","UDP has higher bandwidth","UDP is encrypted","TCP can't carry audio"],"a":0,"w":"A short gap from a lost UDP frame is concealed by the codec; a TCP stall makes a burst of frames miss their play time."},{"n":7,"ti":"UDP","f":"07-udp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Who is responsible for congestion control in a UDP-based protocol?","o":["The kernel's UDP stack","The routers on the path","The application or protocol built on top (QUIC, for example, implements its own)","Nobody: UDP traffic can't cause congestion"],"a":2,"w":"UDP has none. Well-behaved UDP protocols implement it themselves; badly behaved ones flood links."},{"n":7,"ti":"UDP","f":"07-udp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A service's p99 latency has a cluster of requests at about 5.0 seconds. What's a likely UDP-related cause?","o":["TCP slow start","Occasional lost DNS queries, each retried only after the resolver's 5-second default timeout","The garbage collector","A 5-second TTL"],"a":1,"w":"A lost UDP datagram is only noticed when the timeout fires. glibc's default resolver timeout is 5 seconds, so lost DNS queries show up as exactly that."},{"n":8,"ti":"TCP: The Handshake & the Byte Stream","f":"08-tcp-handshake.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A client sends a SYN with seq=7000 . What acknowledgement number does the server's SYN-ACK carry?","o":["7000","0","7001","A random number"],"a":2,"w":"The SYN uses up one sequence number, so the next byte the server expects is 7001."},{"n":8,"ti":"TCP: The Handshake & the Byte Stream","f":"08-tcp-handshake.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"After the handshake, the client sends 300 bytes in a segment with seq=7001 . Which acknowledgement confirms all of them?","o":["ack=7300","ack=7301","ack=7002","ack=300"],"a":1,"w":"The segment carries bytes 7001 to 7300. The acknowledgement names the next byte expected: 7301."},{"n":8,"ti":"TCP: The Handshake & the Byte Stream","f":"08-tcp-handshake.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A sender calls sendall() twice with 50-byte messages. What can the receiver's first recv(4096) return?","o":["Exactly the first 50-byte message","Exactly 100 bytes","Either 50 or 100 bytes, never anything else","Any number of bytes from 1 to 100, in the order they were sent"],"a":3,"w":"Order is guaranteed; boundaries are not. The receiver must find the message ends itself."},{"n":8,"ti":"TCP: The Handshake & the Byte Stream","f":"08-tcp-handshake.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A program's connect() fails instantly with \"Connection refused\". What happened on the wire?","o":["The SYN reached the host, and the host answered with an RST because nothing listens on that port","The SYN was dropped by a firewall","The SYN-ACK was lost","The server's accept queue was full"],"a":0,"w":"A refusal is an answer. Silence, from a dropped SYN, would show up as retries and then a timeout."},{"n":8,"ti":"TCP: The Handshake & the Byte Stream","f":"08-tcp-handshake.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"During a SYN flood, a server using SYN cookies receives a SYN. Where does it keep the state for that half-open connection?","o":["In a larger SYN queue","In the accept queue","Nowhere: the state is encoded in the sequence number of its SYN-ACK and rebuilt from the client's ACK","In a table of trusted addresses"],"a":2,"w":"The cookie is a hash the server can recompute. A forged SYN costs it no memory at all."},{"n":9,"ti":"NAT, Ports & Sockets","f":"09-nat-ports-sockets.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A home router doing NAT forwards a packet from a laptop to a web server. What does it change?","o":["The destination address and port","The source address and source port, recording the mapping in its table","Only the source MAC address","Nothing: it only forwards"],"a":1,"w":"The server sees the router's public address and a port the router chose. The table lets the reply be rewritten back."},{"n":9,"ti":"NAT, Ports & Sockets","f":"09-nat-ports-sockets.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Two devices behind the same NAT both connect from source port 51000 to the same server and port. How does the router keep them apart?","o":["It can't: the second connection fails","By their MAC addresses, which the server echoes back","By using two public IP addresses","It gives each a different public source port"],"a":3,"w":"The public port is the key. Each reply arrives on a different port and maps to a different inside address."},{"n":9,"ti":"NAT, Ports & Sockets","f":"09-nat-ports-sockets.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"An SSH session through a NAT is left idle for an hour. The next keystroke hangs. What is the likely cause?","o":["The NAT's table entry timed out, and neither end was told","The server closed the session politely","The sequence numbers wrapped round","DNS stopped resolving the server"],"a":0,"w":"An expired entry breaks the connection silently. Keep-alives sent more often than the timeout prevent it."},{"n":9,"ti":"NAT, Ports & Sockets","f":"09-nat-ports-sockets.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A service behind a NAT gateway sees connection timeouts to one external API, only at peak load. Which change addresses the usual cause?","o":["Raising the API's rate limit","Switching from TCP to UDP","Reusing connections with a pooled HTTP client, so far fewer NAT ports are in use","Lowering the DNS TTL"],"a":2,"w":"Port exhaustion comes from too many simultaneous or recently closed flows to one destination. Reuse removes most of them."},{"n":9,"ti":"NAT, Ports & Sockets","f":"09-nat-ports-sockets.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does UDP hole punching fail between two \"symmetric\" NATs?","o":["Symmetric NATs block all UDP","Each NAT uses a different public port for each destination, so the port learnt from the STUN server isn't the one the peer must reach","STUN only works over TCP","The two peers have the same private address"],"a":1,"w":"Hole punching needs a predictable public port. Without one, the traffic goes through a relay."},{"n":10,"ti":"DNS Resolution","f":"10-dns-resolution.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A recursive resolver with an empty cache asks a root server for the address of www.example.com . What does the root server send back?","o":["The address","An error, since it isn't responsible for that name","A referral: the name servers for com","The address of example.com's name server"],"a":2,"w":"The root knows only who runs each top-level domain. The resolver follows the referral one level down."},{"n":10,"ti":"DNS Resolution","f":"10-dns-resolution.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which server holds the definitive copy of the records for example.com ?","o":["The authoritative name servers of the example.com zone","The root servers","Your ISP's resolver","8.8.8.8"],"a":0,"w":"Resolvers only hold cached copies. The zone's own servers are the source."},{"n":10,"ti":"DNS Resolution","f":"10-dns-resolution.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A resolver caches an A record with TTL 300 at 12:00:00. The record is changed at 12:01:00. Until when can that resolver return the old address?","o":["12:01:00: changes are pushed to resolvers","12:06:00: five minutes after the change","Until it is restarted","12:05:00: five minutes after it cached the record"],"a":3,"w":"The timer started when the resolver fetched the record. It learns of the change only when it asks again."},{"n":10,"ti":"DNS Resolution","f":"10-dns-resolution.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"dig shows a TTL of 287 for a record whose zone file says 300. What does that tell you?","o":["The zone file is wrong","The answer came from a cache that fetched the record 13 seconds ago","The record expires in 287 minutes","The query took 287 ms"],"a":1,"w":"Caches count the TTL down. A number below the zone's value means you are looking at a cached copy."},{"n":10,"ti":"DNS Resolution","f":"10-dns-resolution.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You run dig new-host.example.com , get NXDOMAIN, then create the record. A minute later the lookup still fails. Why?","o":["The record must have been created wrongly","New records take 24 hours to activate","Your resolver cached the \"no such name\" answer, for the time set in the zone's SOA record","The root servers haven't been updated"],"a":2,"w":"Negative answers are cached too. Ask the authoritative server directly and the record is there."},{"n":11,"ti":"TCP Reliability: ACKs & Retransmission","f":"11-tcp-reliability.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Segments 1 to 6 are sent and segment 3 is lost. What does the receiver send when segments 4, 5 and 6 arrive?","o":["ACKs asking for 5, 6 and 7","Three ACKs that all ask for segment 3","Nothing until segment 3 arrives","A reset"],"a":1,"w":"Acknowledgements are cumulative. With a gap at 3, every later arrival produces the same ACK again: a duplicate."},{"n":11,"ti":"TCP Reliability: ACKs & Retransmission","f":"11-tcp-reliability.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does fast retransmit wait for three duplicate ACKs and not act on the first?","o":["The first two are always lost","It takes three to measure the RTT","The receiver only sends them in threes","Packets can be reordered in transit, which also produces a duplicate ACK or two without any loss"],"a":3,"w":"A segment that is merely a little late shouldn't be resent. Three duplicates make loss the likely explanation."},{"n":11,"ti":"TCP Reliability: ACKs & Retransmission","f":"11-tcp-reliability.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A connection's smoothed RTT is 80 ms with very little variation. Roughly what is its retransmission timeout on Linux?","o":["About 280 ms: the RTT plus a margin that is never less than 200 ms","80 ms","8 ms","Exactly 1 second, always"],"a":0,"w":"The margin is four times the variation, with a 200 ms floor. One second is only the starting value before any measurement."},{"n":11,"ti":"TCP Reliability: ACKs & Retransmission","f":"11-tcp-reliability.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"The final segment of a short HTTP response is lost. Why does the client wait so much longer than one round trip for it?","o":["The server closes the connection first","The client has to ask for it explicitly","No segments follow it, so no duplicate ACKs are generated, and the server must wait for its timer","Final segments are always sent last by the network"],"a":2,"w":"Fast retransmit depends on later arrivals. At the tail there are none, which is what tail loss probes were added for."},{"n":11,"ti":"TCP Reliability: ACKs & Retransmission","f":"11-tcp-reliability.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Two segments from the same burst are lost. What does SACK change?","o":["The receiver stops sending duplicate ACKs","The sender learns about both gaps at once and resends both in one round trip","The timeout is halved","The lost segments are rebuilt by the receiver"],"a":1,"w":"Without SACK the second gap is revealed only by the ACK that follows the first repair, one round trip later."},{"n":12,"ti":"Flow Control & the Receive Window","f":"12-flow-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does the window field in a TCP segment tell the other side?","o":["How congested the network is","How many bytes the sender of the segment still has to send","How much free space the sender of the segment has in its receive buffer","The maximum segment size"],"a":2,"w":"Each side advertises its own free buffer space, and the other side must not exceed it."},{"n":12,"ti":"Flow Control & the Receive Window","f":"12-flow-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A capture shows a server repeatedly advertising win 0 to a client that is uploading. Where is the problem most likely to be?","o":["In the server application, which isn't reading from its socket fast enough","In a congested router on the path","In the client's send buffer","In DNS"],"a":0,"w":"A zero window means data arrived and filled the buffer. Whatever should be draining that buffer is slow."},{"n":12,"ti":"Flow Control & the Receive Window","f":"12-flow-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does a sender facing a zero window send one-byte probes?","o":["To keep the NAT mapping alive","To measure the round-trip time","To make the receiver read faster","Because the window update that reopens the window could be lost, and without probes both sides would wait for ever"],"a":3,"w":"Window updates aren't retransmitted. The ACK of a probe always carries the current window."},{"n":12,"ti":"Flow Control & the Receive Window","f":"12-flow-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Without window scaling, what is the best possible throughput of one TCP connection over a path with a 100 ms round trip?","o":["The bandwidth of the slowest link","About 5 Mbit/s: one 64 KB window per round trip","About 64 Mbit/s","About 100 Mbit/s"],"a":1,"w":"65,535 bytes every 0.1 s is about 5.2 Mbit/s, however fast the links are."},{"n":12,"ti":"Flow Control & the Receive Window","f":"12-flow-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A capture that begins in the middle of a connection shows win 229 . Why may the real window be much larger?","o":["The value is in kilobytes","tcpdump shows the congestion window","The scale factor was announced only in the SYN, which the capture missed, so the field is shown unmultiplied","The window is always doubled by the receiver"],"a":2,"w":"With wscale 7 , 229 means 229 × 128 = 29,312 bytes."},{"n":13,"ti":"Congestion Control: Slow Start, CUBIC & BBR","f":"13-congestion-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Who sets a connection's congestion window, and how?","o":["The receiver, in the window field of each ACK","The sender, by inference from acknowledgements, loss and delay","The routers, in a header they add","The administrator, with a fixed setting"],"a":1,"w":"It lives only in the sender's kernel. The field in the header is the receive window, a different limit."},{"n":13,"ti":"Congestion Control: Slow Start, CUBIC & BBR","f":"13-congestion-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A connection starts with a congestion window of 10 segments and sees no loss. What is the window after three more round trips of slow start?","o":["13","30","40","80"],"a":3,"w":"It doubles each round trip: 10, 20, 40, 80."},{"n":13,"ti":"Congestion Control: Slow Start, CUBIC & BBR","f":"13-congestion-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A Reno sender detects a loss through three duplicate ACKs. Another detects one through a timeout. How do their reactions differ?","o":["The first halves its window and continues; the second drops to one segment and slow-starts","Both halve the window","Both drop to one segment","The first drops to one segment; the second halves"],"a":0,"w":"Duplicate ACKs show the path is still delivering. A timeout suggests it may not be, so the response is harsher."},{"n":13,"ti":"Congestion Control: Slow Start, CUBIC & BBR","f":"13-congestion-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A CUBIC flow over Wi-Fi with 1% random loss gets a small fraction of the link's bandwidth. Why?","o":["Wi-Fi limits TCP by design","The receive window is too small","It treats each random loss as congestion and cuts its window, so the window never grows large","CUBIC only works on wired links"],"a":2,"w":"Loss-based control can't tell interference from a full queue. Throughput falls with the square root of the loss rate."},{"n":13,"ti":"Congestion Control: Slow Start, CUBIC & BBR","f":"13-congestion-control.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A router has a very deep queue, and a loss-based flow is uploading through it. What do other users of that link notice first?","o":["Heavy packet loss","High latency: the flow keeps the queue full, and every packet waits in it","Nothing: deep queues absorb the load","DNS failures"],"a":1,"w":"Bufferbloat. The sender only backs off when the queue overflows, and a deep queue takes a long time to overflow."},{"n":14,"ti":"Closing Connections & TIME_WAIT","f":"14-tcp-teardown-time-wait.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A client sends a FIN and receives an ACK for it. What can the server still do?","o":["Nothing: the connection is closed","Only send its own FIN","Send as much data as it wants, then close its own direction","Reopen the client's direction"],"a":2,"w":"A FIN closes one direction only. The connection is half-closed until the server's FIN."},{"n":14,"ti":"Closing Connections & TIME_WAIT","f":"14-tcp-teardown-time-wait.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"After an orderly close, which side is in TIME_WAIT?","o":["Always the server","The side that sent the first FIN","Both sides","The side that sent the last FIN"],"a":1,"w":"The active closer sends the final ACK, so it must stay around in case that ACK is lost."},{"n":14,"ti":"Closing Connections & TIME_WAIT","f":"14-tcp-teardown-time-wait.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A server shows 20,000 sockets in CLOSE-WAIT , and the number keeps growing. What is the most likely cause?","o":["Clients are attacking it","TIME_WAIT is set too long","The network is dropping FINs","The server program isn't closing its sockets after the clients have closed theirs"],"a":3,"w":"CLOSE-WAIT means \"the peer's FIN has arrived and the local program hasn't called close\". Only the program can leave it."},{"n":14,"ti":"Closing Connections & TIME_WAIT","f":"14-tcp-teardown-time-wait.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A program calls close() on a socket whose receive buffer still holds unread data. What does the kernel send?","o":["An RST, so the peer learns that data was discarded","A FIN, as usual","Nothing until the data is read","The unread data back to the peer"],"a":0,"w":"An orderly FIN would claim everything was received. The reset tells the truth, and the peer sees \"connection reset\"."},{"n":14,"ti":"Closing Connections & TIME_WAIT","f":"14-tcp-teardown-time-wait.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A server is stopped and started again at once, and bind() fails with \"Address already in use\". What fixes it properly?","o":["Waiting is the only option","Closing connections with RST","Setting SO_REUSEADDR on the listening socket before bind()","Choosing a random port each time"],"a":2,"w":"Old connections in TIME_WAIT on that port block a plain bind. The option allows it as long as nothing else is listening."},{"n":15,"ti":"HTTP/1.1","f":"15-http-1-1.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is the Host header mandatory in HTTP/1.1?","o":["It carries the client's own address","One IP address often serves many sites, and the request line's path doesn't say which one is meant","It is needed to open the TCP connection","It tells the server which port to answer on"],"a":1,"w":"The connection is to an IP address. The name the user asked for travels in the request itself."},{"n":15,"ti":"HTTP/1.1","f":"15-http-1-1.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A client's connection breaks before the response arrives. Which request is it unsafe to resend automatically?","o":["GET /orders/17","PUT /orders/17 with the full order","DELETE /orders/17","POST /orders"],"a":3,"w":"The POST may already have created an order. Repeating it could create a second one."},{"n":15,"ti":"HTTP/1.1","f":"15-http-1-1.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does a persistent connection save on the second request?","o":["The TCP and TLS handshakes, and starting again in slow start","The DNS lookup only","The request headers","Nothing unless pipelining is enabled"],"a":0,"w":"The connection is already open and already has a grown congestion window."},{"n":15,"ti":"HTTP/1.1","f":"15-http-1-1.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Three requests are pipelined on one HTTP/1.1 connection. The first takes 2 seconds on the server and the other two take 10 ms. When does the client receive the third response?","o":["After about 10 ms","After about 20 ms","After about 2 seconds: responses must come back in request order","Never: pipelining drops later responses"],"a":2,"w":"Head-of-line blocking. With no identifiers, order is the only way to match responses to requests."},{"n":15,"ti":"HTTP/1.1","f":"15-http-1-1.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does Cache-Control: no-cache tell a browser?","o":["Never store this response","It may store the response but must revalidate it with the server before each use","Cache it for ever","Only cache it in memory"],"a":1,"w":"\"Don't store\" is no-store . With no-cache a 304 can still save the body."},{"n":16,"ti":"HTTP/2","f":"16-http-2.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How does an HTTP/2 client know which request a response frame belongs to?","o":["By the order in which responses arrive","By the TCP port","By the stream ID in every frame's header","By a header named Request-Id"],"a":2,"w":"Every frame names its stream, so frames from different exchanges can be interleaved freely."},{"n":16,"ti":"HTTP/2","f":"16-http-2.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A browser has an HTTP/2 connection to a site and needs 40 files from it. How many TCP connections does it use?","o":["One","Six","Forty","Seven: one for HTML and six for the rest"],"a":0,"w":"All 40 are streams on the same connection, sent at once."},{"n":16,"ti":"HTTP/2","f":"16-http-2.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"On the second request of a connection, HPACK sends the 300-byte cookie header as one byte. How?","o":["It omits cookies after the first request","It gzips the header block","The server remembers cookies by IP address","The field was stored in the connection's dynamic table by the first request, and is now sent as an index into it"],"a":3,"w":"Both sides keep the same table of fields already seen, so a repeat is just a reference."},{"n":16,"ti":"HTTP/2","f":"16-http-2.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"One packet is lost on an HTTP/2 connection carrying ten streams. What happens to the nine streams the packet didn't belong to?","o":["They continue unaffected","They stall too, until the packet is retransmitted, because TCP delivers bytes in order","They are reset","They move to a new connection"],"a":1,"w":"TCP doesn't know about streams. Bytes after the gap wait in the kernel, whoever they are for."},{"n":16,"ti":"HTTP/2","f":"16-http-2.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why was HTTP/2 server push withdrawn from browsers?","o":["It was a security hole","Servers never implemented it","The server can't see the browser's cache, so it often pushed files the browser already had, and gains were small","It required a second connection"],"a":2,"w":"A hint that lets the browser decide (103 Early Hints) does the useful part without the waste."},{"n":17,"ti":"TLS 1.3 & Certificates","f":"17-tls-1-3.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"On a new connection, how many round trips does the TLS 1.3 handshake add before the client can send its request?","o":["None","One","Two","Four"],"a":1,"w":"ClientHello out, ServerHello and certificate back, and the request goes with the client's Finished. TCP's handshake is a separate round trip before it."},{"n":17,"ti":"TLS 1.3 & Certificates","f":"17-tls-1-3.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"An attacker records an encrypted session today and steals the server's certificate key a year later. Why can't they decrypt the recording?","o":["The certificate will have expired","The recording is too old to parse","The certificate key is stored on the client","The session keys came from ephemeral key pairs that were discarded; the certificate key was only used to sign"],"a":3,"w":"Forward secrecy. The long-term key authenticates the handshake and doesn't protect the session keys."},{"n":17,"ti":"TLS 1.3 & Certificates","f":"17-tls-1-3.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What stops an attacker in the middle from doing a separate key exchange with each side?","o":["The server signs the handshake with the private key of a certificate that chains to a root the client trusts","The key exchange is encrypted with a password","TCP sequence numbers","The client's IP address is checked"],"a":0,"w":"The attacker can't produce that signature for the real server's name, so the client sees a certificate error."},{"n":17,"ti":"TLS 1.3 & Certificates","f":"17-tls-1-3.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A site loads in Chrome, but curl reports \"unable to get local issuer certificate\". What is the most likely cause?","o":["The certificate has expired","curl doesn't support TLS 1.3","The server isn't sending its intermediate certificate, which the browser fetched or had cached","The host name is wrong"],"a":2,"w":"Without the intermediate, curl can't link the server's certificate to any root it has."},{"n":17,"ti":"TLS 1.3 & Certificates","f":"17-tls-1-3.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why should 0-RTT early data carry only idempotent requests?","o":["It is sent unencrypted","An attacker can capture the first flight and replay it, and the server can't tell","It is limited to 100 bytes","It skips TCP"],"a":1,"w":"Nothing fresh from the server is mixed into early data, so a copy is as valid as the original."},{"n":18,"ti":"HTTP/3 & QUIC","f":"18-http-3-quic.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why was QUIC built on UDP and not as a new protocol directly on IP, or as a change to TCP?","o":["UDP is faster than TCP","UDP is encrypted","Firewalls and NATs pass UDP but drop unknown protocols and unfamiliar TCP behaviour, and TCP in kernels takes years to change","UDP has built-in streams"],"a":2,"w":"Deployability. QUIC supplies the reliability, streams and encryption itself, in user-space code."},{"n":18,"ti":"HTTP/3 & QUIC","f":"18-http-3-quic.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"An HTTP/3 connection carries streams 0, 4 and 8. A packet holding data only for stream 4 is lost. What happens?","o":["Stream 4 waits for the retransmission; streams 0 and 8 are delivered as they arrive","All three streams wait","Stream 4 is reset","The connection is re-established"],"a":0,"w":"Each stream is ordered independently, which is the fix for HTTP/2's transport-level blocking."},{"n":18,"ti":"HTTP/3 & QUIC","f":"18-http-3-quic.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How many round trips pass before a brand-new QUIC connection can send its HTTP request?","o":["None","One","Two","Three"],"a":1,"w":"The transport and TLS handshakes are combined. TCP followed by TLS 1.3 needs two."},{"n":18,"ti":"HTTP/3 & QUIC","f":"18-http-3-quic.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A phone moves from Wi-Fi to mobile data during a download over HTTP/3. Why does the download continue?","o":["The phone keeps its Wi-Fi address","UDP has no connections to break","The server stores the download for later","The connection is identified by a connection ID, so packets from the new address are still recognised"],"a":3,"w":"The server validates the new path and continues. A TCP connection, tied to its addresses, would be dead."},{"n":18,"ti":"HTTP/3 & QUIC","f":"18-http-3-quic.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A company firewall blocks all UDP except DNS. What does a browser inside do when it visits a site that offers HTTP/3?","o":["It fails to load the site","It tunnels QUIC inside DNS","It uses HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1 over TCP","It asks the user to open the port"],"a":2,"w":"Browsers race QUIC against TCP and fall back silently. That is why TCP support must stay."}];
